---
import DocsLayout from "../../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const requirements = `"requirements": {
  "access": "full_collection",
  "contracts": [],
  "capabilities": {
    "contract_version": 2,
    "required": ["collection.read", "records.edit"],
    "optional": ["records.create"]
  },
  "files": {
    "required": ["list", "read", "replace", "move"],
    "optional": ["add"],
    "scope": { "kind": "selected_folders", "folders": ["attachments"] }
  }
}`;

const check = `const { capabilities, role, fileFolders } = db.hello.grant;

newEntryButton.hidden = !capabilities.includes("records.create");

try {
  await db.create({ type: "task", frontmatter: { title } });
} catch (e) {
  if (isMdbaseError(e, "forbidden") || isMdbaseError(e, "unauthenticated")) {
    showReconnectPrompt(); // recovery: "reauthorize"
  } else {
    throw e;
  }
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Authorization"
  description="Your app gets a grant: access to one collection with the capabilities the user approved. Ask for what you use, and check what you got."
  active="authorization"
  headings={[
    { href: "#flows", label: "Grants" },
    { href: "#capabilities", label: "Capabilities" },
    { href: "#files", label: "Files" },
    { href: "#step-up", label: "Check what you got" },
    { href: "#private", label: "Private collections" },
    { href: "#disconnect", label: "Revocation" }
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="flows">Grants</h2>
  <p>
    When a user connects your app to a collection, mdbase records a
    {" "}<strong>grant</strong>: your app, one collection, the capabilities the
    user approved, and your app’s session key. The replica checks the grant
    on every session.
  </p>
  <p>
    A grant never exceeds the user’s own role in the collection. A viewer
    can’t give your app write access. When the grant or the role doesn’t allow
    a request, it fails with <code>forbidden</code>; <code>details</code>
    {" "}names the capability.
  </p>

  <h2 id="capabilities">Capabilities</h2>
  <p>
    Declare capabilities in your manifest. The user can’t turn off a
    {" "}<code>required</code> one without declining your app. Each
    {" "}<code>optional</code> one gets its own switch; destructive ones start
    switched off.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={requirements} language="json" label="Manifest fragment" />
  <table class="api-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Capability</th>
        <th>Allows</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>collection.read</code></td><td>Describe the collection; read, query and watch records; list and run saved views; read types; validate</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>records.create</code></td><td>Create records</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>records.edit</code></td><td>Update and rename records</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>records.delete</code></td><td>Delete records</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>views.manage</code></td><td>Create, update and delete saved views</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>definitions.manage</code></td><td>Create and update types; check and apply type packs</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>background.schedule</code></td><td>Timers; see <a href="/sdk/notifications/">Notifications and timers</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>offline.replica</code></td><td>Keep a replica of the collection in the app. Not granted to other apps at launch.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A manifest without <code>capabilities</code> asks for read-only access.
  </p>

  <h2 id="files">Files</h2>
  <p>
    Files that aren’t records, such as images and PDFs, are requested under
    {" "}<code>requirements.files</code>. Each file action pairs with a record
    capability, and you must declare both halves:
  </p>
  <table class="api-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Capability</th>
        <th>File actions</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>collection.read</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>read</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>records.create</code></td><td><code>add</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>records.edit</code></td><td><code>replace</code>, <code>move</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>records.delete</code></td><td><code>delete</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The scope is the whole collection
    {" "}(<code>&#123; "kind": "collection" &#125;</code>) or named folders. Ask
    for folders when you can.
  </p>

  <h2 id="step-up">Check what you got</h2>
  <p>
    The user may approve less than you asked for. Read the grant from
    {" "}<code>db.hello</code> and hide what your app can’t do.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={check} language="ts" label="grant.ts" />

  <h2 id="private">Private collections</h2>
  <p>
    In a Private collection, mdbase can’t read the content, and it can’t
    decide on its own that your app may. The user must also approve the grant
    on one of their devices, and can narrow it there. Until they do, your app
    can’t reach the collection.
  </p>

  <h2 id="disconnect">Revocation</h2>
  <p>
    The user can revoke your app’s access at any time. Open sessions close
    straight away, and requests fail with <code>unauthenticated</code>. Treat
    both <code>unauthenticated</code> and <code>forbidden</code> as a reason to
    ask the user to reconnect, never to retry in a loop.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
